::: {.doc-subject}

Remove all self-loops from the graph.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L3492-L3616){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.remove_selfloops()
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Remove self-loops](../assets/graphs/remove-selfloops.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Before: a four-node cycle with a self-loop on B, in the accent color, and a pair of parallel edges between C and D. After: the self-loop is gone, shown dashed, while both parallel C to D edges remain." width="100%"}

A self-loop is an edge where the source and target nodes are the same. This method removes
all such edges from the graph, which is a common preprocessing step for many graph
algorithms that assume simple graphs.

Self-loops often appear as artifacts of data collection (a user replying to their own post,
a page linking to itself, a gene interacting with itself) and can distort degree counts,
clustering coefficients, and similar metrics. Every self-loop is removed, including
multiple loops on the same node, while all nodes are kept. The graph is modified in place;
if any loops were removed, the change is recorded as a single step in the step log (see
`steps`). On a graph without self-loops the call does nothing.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Self for method chaining.
:::

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

The removal works uniformly for directed and undirected graphs and touches only
self-loops (edges of the form $(v, v)$), so parallel edges between distinct
endpoints are left in place. If your goal is a fully simple graph, chain this with
`remove_duplicate_edges()` or call `simplify()` in one step. To inspect what you're
about to lose first, `which_loops()` returns the offending edge IDs and
`count_loops()` gives their per-node totals.

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~which_loops`) 

:   The IDs of the self-loops.

[](`~remove_duplicate_edges`) 

:   Remove parallel edges.

[](`~simplify`) 

:   Remove both self-loops and parallel edges.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

A self-loop connects a node to itself; here two of the three edges are self-loops on
"A" and "B", which `which_loops()` reports by edge ID. `remove_selfloops()` strips both:

::: {#b07621ee .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

g = ec.Graph.from_edges([("A", "B"), ("A", "A"), ("B", "B")])
loops = g.which_loops()
g.remove_selfloops()

loops, g.count_edges(), g.which_loops()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
([2, 3], 1, [])
```
:::
:::


Parallel edges between different nodes are not affected. Here the duplicated X-Y edge
survives, so the graph is still not simple; `simplify()` would remove both kinds:

::: {#0960b6b2 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
g = ec.Graph.from_edges([("X", "Y"), ("X", "Y"), ("Y", "Y")])
g.remove_selfloops()

g.count_edges(), g.is_simple()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
(2, False)
```
:::
:::


### Transfers between one's own accounts

In a bank's export, money that a customer moves between their own accounts has the same
payer and payee, and becomes a self-loop. Each self-loop adds 2 to its node's degree, so
Dee and Eve look busier than they are:

::: {#c3297a3b .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
export = [
    ("Acme", "Dee", 2600),
    ("Acme", "Eve", 2700),
    ("Dee", "Grocer", 64),
    ("Dee", "Dee", 500),
    ("Eve", "Grocer", 38),
    ("Eve", "Eve", 300),
    ("Dee", "Eve", 80),
]

bank = ec.Graph.from_edges(export, directed=True)
before = bank.get_degree("Dee"), bank.get_degree("Eve")

bank.remove_selfloops()

before, (bank.get_degree("Dee"), bank.get_degree("Eve"))
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}
```
((5, 5), (3, 3))
```
:::
:::


